window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-25","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-25-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-01/","title_he":"א – איסור בישול בשר בחלב","title_en":"The Prohibition of Cooking Meat and Milk","body_en":"<p>The Torah forbade cooking meat with milk, as it is stated: \"You shall not cook a kid in its mother's milk.\" This prohibition is mentioned three times in the Torah (Shemot 23:19; 34:26; Devarim 14:21), to teach us that the prohibition has three parts: 1) It is forbidden to cook them together. 2) If they were cooked together, it is forbidden to eat them. 3) If they were cooked together, it is also forbidden to derive benefit from them (Ḥullin 115b; SA YD 87:1).</p><p>This prohibition is unique and different from the other prohibitions of eating, for all the forbidden foods are always forbidden, whereas meat on its own is kosher and milk on its own is kosher, and cooking them together is forbidden, and if they were cooked together – they become forbidden with an especially severe prohibition. For all the other forbidden foods, such as the meat of impure domesticated and wild animals, a Jew is permitted to cook for a non-Jew, but meat and milk a Jew is forbidden to cook for a non-Jew. It is even forbidden to assist in cooking them, for example by raising the flame on which they are cooking. In addition, meat and milk that were cooked together are forbidden for benefit, and therefore it is forbidden to sell them to a non-Jew, and it is even forbidden to give them to him as a gift, since the Jew derives benefit from this. And even if one burns them, it is forbidden to benefit from the heat of their burning or from the ash that results from them. It is even forbidden to place them before ownerless animals, since the Jew derives satisfaction from this. Rather, one must bury the forbidden food, and when it is difficult to bury it, one may throw it into a field or into the garbage bin. And although it is likely that animals will eat from it, there is no prohibition in this, since he does not intend this.<sup>1</sup></p><p>By Torah law the prohibition is to cook meat with milk, and without cooking there is no prohibition. Therefore, it is permitted to mix them together without cooking for the needs of a non-Jew or for any other need. The Sages made a fence around the Torah and forbade a Jew to eat meat and milk together even without cooking (SA YD 87:1; 91:8), and the Sages reinforced this fence with many prohibitions: they forbade eating one kind when the other kind is placed on the table (below, halakha 3), and they forbade baking meat or dairy bread lest it be eaten with the other kind (below, halakha 4). They even went further and forbade eating milk after meat without a significant wait, and forbade eating meat after milk without cleaning the mouth (as explained in the next chapter). The reason for all these decrees is explained below (halakha 5).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Mishna Temura 33b: \"And these are the things that are buried… and meat in milk.\" They did not say to burn them, so that one will not benefit from their burning and from their ash. The Issur Ve-heter He-arokh (21:12) wrote that it is forbidden to place meat and milk that were cooked together before an ownerless animal, and so wrote many poskim, because a person derives benefit from sustaining animals (MA 448:9). Some say that it is permitted to leave them in a field in a place where animals can come and eat them, since the prohibition is to place it before them (MA 445:2; Ḥok Ya'akov, Eliyahu Rabba, Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav). And some say that this too is forbidden; rather, the solution is to bury them or to throw them into the toilet or into a river in such a way that no creature will benefit from it (Issur Ve-heter He-arokh 21:13; Knesset Ha-gedola, Ḥokhmat Adam). The Rav Pe'alim (3, YD 5) explained that the intention is a river that has no fish in it, or that one should crumble the forbidden food until fish cannot eat it. Thus far. However, this is difficult, since in any case animals will benefit from them, for even if he crumbles the forbidden food – tiny fish will benefit from it, and if he buries it – worms and insects will benefit from it, and it improves the soil and brings benefit to man. Rather, apparently their intention is that since this benefit is not so noticeable, there is no prohibition in it. If so, as long as one does not intend to feed animals with the forbidden food and does not benefit from it, one may rely on the lenient opinions and throw the forbidden food into the garbage bin or into a field; however, one who benefits from this must bury it.<br>Since the prohibition of meat and milk is stated in the language \"you shall not cook,\" the Torah prohibition applies only to the normal manner of cooking, and without cooking there is no prohibition in mixing meat with milk; but the Sages forbade eating meat and milk that were mixed even without cooking (Pesaḥim 44b). Some say that frying and roasting are also considered cooking, whose prohibition is from the Torah (Pri Ḥadash and Pleiti), and some say that their prohibition is rabbinic, since they are not like cooking, which mixes the flavours into one another thoroughly (Pri Toar, Arukh Ha-shulḥan).<br>As a rule, all Torah prohibitions forbid benefit only in the normal manner of deriving benefit; however, meat and milk and kilei ha-kerem (mixed plantings of the vineyard) are forbidden by Torah law even not in the normal manner of benefit (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 14:10; SA 155:3).<br>One who cooks meat and milk or eats from them is punished with lashes, provided that he did so deliberately before witnesses who warned him, and that they contained at least the measure of a kezayit (Ḥullin 108b; Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 9:1). There is a dispute as to whether one who derives benefit is liable to lashes. One who ate less than a kezayit or cooked less than a kezayit has transgressed a Torah prohibition but does not receive lashes (Ben Ish Ḥai, Year 2, Beha'alotekha 2). One who cooks them even with partial cooking transgresses a Torah prohibition, but only if he prepared them for eating like the food of Ben Drusai (cooked to about one-third done) does he receive lashes (the opinion of most poskim, among them: Pri Megadim, Pleiti, Zivḥei Tzedek, Ben Ish Ḥai). One who cooks in a kli rishon (the vessel that was on the fire) – it is forbidden by Torah law. Pouring from a kli rishon, which according to halakha cooks to the depth of a peel: according to the Pri Ḥadash it is forbidden by Torah law, and according to the Pri Toar it is rabbinic (see below 35:3)."}]},{"slug":"17-25-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-02/","title_he":"ב – הבשר והחלב שבכלל האיסור","title_en":"The Meat and Milk Included in the Prohibition","body_en":"<p>The Tanna'im disagreed about which kinds of meat and milk are included in the Torah prohibition. According to Rabbi Akiva, the meat and milk that were forbidden are those of one of the three species of pure domesticated animals (behema): cattle, sheep and goats. However, the seven species of pure wild animals (ḥaya), such as the deer and the ayal, are not included in the Torah prohibition. This law too we learned from the fact that the Torah repeated the prohibition \"You shall not cook a kid in its mother's milk\" three times, to teach us that the three species of pure domesticated animals are included in the prohibition, whereas the species of pure wild animals, pure birds, and impure domesticated and wild animals are not included in the prohibition (Mishna Ḥullin 113a). The difference between domesticated and wild animals is that domesticated animals are tame and wild animals are not tame. Since domesticated animals are in a person's possession, he can milk them and slaughter their offspring, and the Torah forbade mixing the meat of one of the domesticated animals with the milk of one of the domesticated animals. The Torah gave the example of a young goat in its mother's milk, because the goat's milk is plentiful, and it often gives birth to two kids, and after the kids had grown a little, people would slaughter one in order to eat its meat, and since its mother continued to nurse the second kid, it was common for them to cook the kid's meat in its mother's milk (Rashbam, Shemot 23:19).</p><p>According to Rabbi Yossi Ha-gelili, the seven species of pure wild animals are also included in the Torah prohibition, since they too have meat and milk that are permitted to be eaten, but fowl is not included in the prohibition, since the female bird has no milk. And some say that fowl meat is also included in the Torah prohibition, since it too is a kind of meat. The proof is that it must be slaughtered in order to permit its meat.</p><p>In practice, the halakha was ruled according to Rabbi Akiva, that the Torah prohibition applies only to species of domesticated animals, and by rabbinic law it is forbidden to eat wild animals and fowl with milk as well, but it is permitted to cook them for a non-Jew and permitted to derive benefit from them (SA YD 87:2).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"<b>Fowl in milk</b>: According to Rabbi Yossi Ha-gelili, even by rabbinic law there is no prohibition to eat fowl in milk. On the other hand, the opinion of the Tanna Kamma in the Mishna (Ḥullin 103b) is that wild animals and fowl are also forbidden by Torah law, and there are Rishonim and Aḥaronim who hold that the halakha follows him (Tosafot, Rabbeinu Peretz, Hagahot Sha'arei Dura, Issur Ve-heter He-arokh, Maharshal and Baḥ). However, according to the overwhelming majority of Rishonim and Aḥaronim, the halakha follows Rabbi Akiva, and wild animals and fowl are forbidden to be eaten by rabbinic law, and are permitted for cooking and benefit (Rosh 8:51, in the name of the Rif; Rid, Rashba, Ra'ah, Orḥot Ḥayim, Rambam, Tur and SA 87:3; Shakh, and nearly all the Aḥaronim). The Rambam added and wrote (Hilkhot Mamrim 2:9) that one who says that fowl in milk is forbidden by Torah law transgresses \"bal tosif\" (the prohibition of adding to the Torah). Regarding cooking and benefit of fowl and wild animals in milk, some are stringent, and even among the poskim who hold that the entire basis of the prohibition of eating them is rabbinic, some are stringent to forbid them in cooking and benefit as well, by rabbinic law. However, according to most poskim there is no prohibition of cooking or benefit with them.<br>Hard skin, sinews, hard bones and hooves are not considered meat, since people do not usually eat them. But it is forbidden to cook them with milk by rabbinic law (SA 87:7). A calf that nursed milk and was afterwards slaughtered, and milk was found in its stomach: according to the Rif, Rambam and SA (87:9), it does not have the status of milk, and it is permitted to cook it with meat. And according to Tosafot, Rabbeinu Tam, Rosh, Rashba and Rema, if it is liquid, it has the status of milk and it is forbidden by Torah law to cook it with meat, and if it has congealed, it is permitted to cook it with meat. And some say that even congealed milk may not be cooked with meat (Rashi, Maharshal)."}]},{"slug":"17-25-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-03/","title_he":"ג – אכילה על שולחן אחד","title_en":"Eating at One Table","body_en":"<p>The Sages enacted that when a person eats a meat food, no dairy food should be placed on his table, and when he eats a dairy food, no meat food should be placed on his table, so that he will not mistakenly eat them together. And not only the meat of domesticated animals is included in the prohibition, but also the meat of fowl and wild animals (Ḥullin 103b; 104b; SA 88:1). But one who has eaten meat is permitted to prepare dairy foods before six hours have passed for him. For the enactment was intended to serve as a fence against eating the two kinds together, and not to safeguard the waiting period between eating meat and eating milk (see Arukh Ha-shulḥan 11 and Darkhei Teshuva 16).</p><p>The Sages also enacted that two people who know each other should not eat at one table, this one meat and that one milk, lest one come to eat from his fellow's food and thus be found eating meat with milk. But they did not forbid two strangers who happened to come to one place to eat at one table, this one meat and that one milk, because a stranger is not accustomed to take food from his neighbour (Ḥullin 107b). If the two people who know each other place a sign between them that will remind them to keep apart from each other's food, they may eat together at one table. As a sign, one may place a utensil or food that is not usually placed there, and likewise one may spread a separate tablecloth for one of them (SA 88:2). And if they sit far from each other, so that neither can stretch out his hand and reach his fellow's food, they are permitted to eat at one table without a sign (Yam Shel Shlomo and Baḥ). One who has finished eating meat may sit next to his fellow who is eating milk and eat pareve foods, even though six hours have not yet passed for him.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Some permit, by means of a sign, even an individual to eat meat at a table on which there is milk (Ḥokhmat Adam 40:11). And many forbid this, because a sign helps only for two people, for if one forgets the sign, his fellow will remember and prevent him from the prohibition (Hafla'a; see Badei Ha-shulḥan 88:14). One who eats dairy foods may have raw meat on his table, since there is no concern that he will come to eat it. But ready-to-eat food placed in a bag may not be on the table, lest he take it out, unless it is covered and wrapped and taking it out involves considerable effort (see SA OḤ 451:1; MB 8)."}]},{"slug":"17-25-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-04/","title_he":"ד – אפיית פת חלבית או בשרית","title_en":"Baking Dairy or Meat Bread","body_en":"<p>The Sages decreed that one should not knead bread dough with milk, lest people forget that the bread is dairy and come to eat it with meat. Likewise they decreed that one should not knead it with meat fat, lest people forget that the bread is meaty and come to eat it with milk. If one erred and kneaded bread dough with milk or with meat fat, the Sages decreed that the bread is forbidden to be eaten (Pesaḥim 30a-b).</p><p>The prohibition is to prepare bread that is eaten over the course of more than one meal, but it is permitted to prepare dairy or meat bread in a quantity that the household will finish at the next meal, since there is no concern that by the next meal they will forget that it is dairy or meaty.</p><p>Likewise it is permitted to prepare dairy or meat bread when one changes the bread's shape, in such a way that the household will understand that its status is different and will find out whether it is dairy or meaty. But such a sign does not permit baking dairy or meat bread in order to sell it, lest there be people who will not notice the sign. Only when one makes a clear sign from which everyone understands that this bread is dairy or meaty is it permitted, such as a pita with yellow cheese or a strip of meat stuck on it (Pesaḥim 36a; SA 97:1).</p><p>The prohibition applies specifically to bread, which people are accustomed to eat with dairy or meat foods. The same applies to crackers, which are sometimes eaten with cheese and sometimes with sausage. But cakes and other baked goods may be prepared dairy or meaty, since they are generally not eaten with meat or milk (Maharit 2, YD 18).<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Pesaḥim 36a: \"It was taught: One may not knead dough with milk, and if one kneaded – all the bread is forbidden because of habituation to sin.\" The Gemara there explained that it is permitted to knead with milk \"like the eye of an ox.\" Rashi explains: a small amount, such that one eats it \"immediately, all at once,\" so that there is no concern that he will forget that it is dairy. And according to the Rambam, the meaning is that it has a noticeable change that will prevent a mishap. In practice, both explanations were accepted, and one of them is sufficient to permit.<br>The SA (97:1) wrote following Rashi: \"And if it was a small amount, enough to be eaten at once… it is permitted.\" They explained that the intention is that he will eat all of it at the next meal (Pri Megadim, Darkhei Teshuva and Kaf Ha-ḥayim). However, the Rema seemingly permitted kneading dairy or meat bread in a quantity that will be finished within a day and night, that is, dairy bread that will be finished during the holiday of Shavu'ot and meat bread that will be finished on Shabbat. His source is the Hagahot Sha'arei Dura, cited in the Beit Yosef. So wrote the Pri Ḥadash, Ḥokhmat Adam, Ḥavot Da'at and Arukh Ha-shulḥan. It is possible to explain that they do not disagree: on weekdays it is permitted only for the next meal, whereas for Shabbat and festivals it is permitted even for a day and night, since the preparations for them are made with planning and forethought, and people remember better whether the bread is dairy or meaty. In this way we rule in accordance with both opinions, as is implied in the Beit Yosef.<br>If one erred and prepared a large amount of bread that became forbidden, according to most poskim it is forbidden to divide it among many people, giving each one the amount for his next meal (Pri Megadim, Siftei Da'at 1; Ḥavot Da'at, Bi'urim 3). Some say that even if one throws away everything except the amount for one meal, the bread remaining for the next meal is forbidden, since it has already become forbidden (Ḥamudei Daniel 12). In my humble opinion, one may be lenient in this. According to most poskim it is permitted to sell the forbidden bread to a non-Jew (Pri Toar 3; Zivḥei Tzedek 19, Darkhei Teshuva, Rav Pe'alim).<br>Regarding the sign, the Rashba wrote (Torat Ha-bayit Ha-katzar 3:4) that by means of it people will remember to ask whether the bread is dairy or meaty. However, a sign that is recognised by the household is not recognised by other people, and therefore the Aḥaronim wrote that one should not prepare dairy bread with a sign in order to sell it (Ḥavot Da'at, based on Maharit 2, YD 18, and many others). Some were inclined to be lenient in a pressing situation even for sale, when the sign is noticeable (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 97:8; Ben Ish Ḥai, Year 2, Shelaḥ 17). One may be lenient in accordance with them with a clear sign that is evident to all, for in such a case it is reasonable that even the stringent opinions would be lenient."}]},{"slug":"17-25-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-05/","title_he":"ה – טעם הגזירות הנוספות בבשר וחלב","title_en":"The Reason for the Additional Decrees Concerning Meat and Milk","body_en":"<p>It is fitting to ask: is it not a well-known rule that the Sages do not enact a decree upon a decree, whereas in the prohibition of eating meat at a table on which there is milk, and likewise in the prohibition of baking dairy or meat bread, they enacted a decree upon a decree? For only meat and milk that were cooked together are forbidden to be eaten by Torah law, and the Sages decreed that one may not eat meat with milk even if they were not cooked together; why then did they go further and decree a decree upon a decree, forbidding even bringing meat and milk together onto the table during the meal, and likewise forbidding baking dairy or meat bread?</p><p>The commentators explained that with regard to the prohibition of meat and milk the concern of a mishap is especially great, since meat on its own is permitted and milk on its own is permitted, and both are found in the kitchen, and they can easily become mixed, and therefore it was necessary to enact decrees upon decrees concerning them. Furthermore, had the Sages not added decrees in this matter, even the basic decrees would not have been observed; therefore all the decrees are considered one decree intended to fortify the separation between meat and milk.</p><p>Not only that, but in all the decrees concerning eating, they equated the meat of fowl and wild animals, whose prohibition is rabbinic, with the meat of domesticated animals, whose prohibition is from the Torah, because if people were lenient with fowl or wild animals, they would mistakenly be lenient with the meat of domesticated animals as well.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"In the Gemara (Ḥullin 104a-b), they asked how they could have decreed that it is forbidden to bring fowl with cheese onto the table, since this is a decree upon a decree. Rav Yosef learned from this that the Mishna follows the opinion of the Tanna'im who hold that the prohibition of cooking fowl in milk is from the Torah. However, from the words of Abaye it emerges that the Mishna holds even according to Rabbi Akiva, that since the concern of a mishap is great, and fowl resembles the meat of domesticated animals, they decreed a decree upon a decree and forbade fowl as well. So it was ruled in the Rambam and SA 88:1, and Taz 1. Furthermore, since the concern of mixture between meat and milk is great, all the decrees are considered one decree intended to establish the absolute separation between meat and milk. So explained the Rashba and Ran. And we likewise find that the Torah too was very stringent with the prohibition of meat and milk, forbidding it even for benefit. Just as the prohibition of bringing onto one table while eating applies to fowl and milk, so too the law of baking bread with fowl fat (Pri Megadim 97, Siftei Da'at 1), and of waiting between meat and milk (SA 89:1; below 26, note 3). For otherwise great confusion would arise, as explained above regarding the reason for the decrees. It is also possible that they took into account the opinion of the Tanna'im who hold that wild animals and fowl too are forbidden by Torah law (halakha 2, note 2). And so it is proper to practise in actual fact regarding wiping the mouth between cheese and fowl (below 26:1, note 1). However, with regard to the prohibition of cooking and benefit, they did not decree concerning wild animals and fowl.<br>The Sages did not enact decrees concerning other forbidden foods, because there is no concern that a Jew will come to eat them, since they are entirely forbidden to him (Shakh 88:2). However, with foods in which the prohibition is not recognisable, such as orla fruit, one must be careful (see Pri Toar 88:1)."}]},{"slug":"17-25-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-06/","title_he":"ו – הפרדה בין מאכלי בשר וחלב","title_en":"Separation Between Meat and Dairy Foods","body_en":"<p>One who wishes to eat meat at a table on which milk was eaten, or vice versa, must clean the table of the remnants of the previous food, and without this it is forbidden to eat the other kind at that table. Likewise it is forbidden to eat meat on a tablecloth on which dairy foods were eaten, or vice versa, unless it is clean of the remnants of the previous food (SA 89:4). If the tablecloth is made of fabric that absorbs dirt, it must be laundered. And if it is made of plastic that does not absorb dirt, one may clean it with a damp cloth. Even when one is careful to place the foods inside plates, one must clean the table between eating meat and milk.</p><p>Bread that was served at a meat meal, if there is a reasonable concern that people touched it with hands that had fat or meat gravy on them, should not be served at a dairy meal, so that people will not come to eat remnants of meat with milk, and likewise the reverse (SA 89:4). And if people were careful not to touch the bread with hands to which fat or gravy was stuck, it is permitted to eat it at a dairy meal (see SA 91:3).</p><p>Salads that were served at a meat meal, if there is a reasonable concern that the diners took from them with cutlery that had meat remnants on it, should not be served at a dairy meal, and likewise the reverse. And if the salads have their own serving spoons, and there is no concern that one of the diners took from them with cutlery bearing meat remnants, it is permitted to serve them at a dairy meal, and likewise the reverse. Some are stringent le-khatḥila (from the outset), that no foods served at a meat meal should be served at a dairy meal, and likewise the reverse. And even those who are meticulous about this may be lenient when necessary, as long as there is no reasonable concern that remnants of milk or meat stuck to the salad or the bread.<sup>6</sup></p><p>As we learned (in halakha 3), while eating meat it is forbidden for milk to be on the table, and likewise the reverse, lest people mistakenly eat them together. But not at the time of eating, it is permitted to place them on one table (Ḥullin 104b; SA 88:1). Likewise it is permitted to place pieces of meat and cheese in one bag, or in one drawer in a cupboard or in the refrigerator, while taking care that they do not touch each other. And if they touched each other, one rinses the place where they touched and it is permitted to eat them (Ḥullin 107b; SA 91:1-2; below 35:1).</p><p>Similarly, one should not place milk bags in a place from which they may drip onto meat foods, and if it dripped, one must rinse the meat until not a trace of the milk's taste remains in it, and then it is permitted to eat it and cook it.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"When the diners took from the central salad with their own cutlery, and there is a reasonable concern that meat gravy was stuck to the forks, and if we forbid serving the salad at a dairy meal a loss will be caused – it is permitted to mix the salad well and serve it at a dairy meal, for even if there was a little meat taste in the salad, through the mixing it will be nullified in sixty and in no part of the salad will it be possible to sense a meat taste. And although \"one may not nullify a prohibition le-khatḥila,\" here we are not dealing with a prohibition. In addition, we have no certainty that there was a meat taste here that was nullified."},{"n":7,"en":"In Ḥullin 112a, they forbade placing a jar of salt next to a jar of kutaḥ (a dairy sauce), lest some of the kutaḥ fall into the salt, and afterwards they would use the dairy salt for a meat dish. And if one did place them so, it does not become forbidden (SA and Rema 95:5). Tosafot (s.v. \"mahu\") wrote that one should not forbid placing a jar of meat next to a jar of milk in one box, since in this people are careful, and only regarding kutaḥ in salt are people not careful, since there is no prohibition in some of the kutaḥ falling into the salt. And so it was ruled in SA 95:6. However, according to the Rema, if the meat and the milk are uncovered and close to each other – le-khatḥila one should be stringent. And according to the Maharshal (Yam Shel Shlomo 8:66), one must be stringent by law."}]},{"slug":"17-25-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-07/","title_he":"ז – הפרדה בין הכלים","title_en":"Separation Between the Utensils","body_en":"<p>In a kitchen where dairy and meat foods are prepared, one must designate separate cooking utensils for milk and separate cooking utensils for meat, and it is proper that there be a noticeable difference between them, so that they will not be mistakenly interchanged. Likewise one must designate a set of plates and cutlery for dairy foods and a set of plates and cutlery for meat foods, and it is proper that there be a noticeable difference between them, so that they will not be interchanged. It is also proper to assign each kind a special place so that they will not become mixed. Even when eating cold meat or dairy foods, the custom of Israel is to maintain the separation between the utensils, and to eat and serve dairy foods in dairy utensils and meat foods in meat utensils, in order to prevent mishaps and mixing between them.</p><p>Many are accustomed to designate two salt shakers, one for dairy meals and one for meat meals. For sometimes the salt shaker touches the foods, and sometimes people touch the salt shaker with hands that have gravy on them, and if one salt shaker is used for both kinds, there is a concern that through it a trace of taste of one kind will become mixed into the other. And if one wishes to use one salt shaker for meat and dairy, one must take care that it be clean, and if it became soiled with a trace of food, wipe it well.</p><p>Those who use bottles containing sauces or ketchup for both dairy and meat meals must take care that the nozzle does not touch the foods, so that nothing from the dairy or meat foods will stick to it. Likewise they must take care not to touch these containers with hands to which a trace of moisture from meat or dairy foods is stuck. And if they were not careful about this, one must wipe the container and its nozzle, so that a trace of taste of one kind will not reach the other through them.</p><p>The tray of a baby's high chair: since generally one does not place on it food that is yad soledet bo (hot enough that the hand recoils from it), so as not to harm the baby, there is no concern that the tray will absorb the taste of meat or milk, and all that is necessary is to clean the tray well between meals. However, one who sometimes places on the tray food that is yad soledet bo must determine that the tray will be meat or dairy, and if he wishes to place on it something of the other kind, he should clean the tray beforehand and take care that the food of the other kind does not reach the heat of yad soledet bo.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-25-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-08/","title_he":"ח – מאכלי פרווה","title_en":"Pareve Foods","body_en":"<p>A pareve food is a food that is neither dairy nor meaty. Le-khatḥila one should maintain the separation between the kinds, and when one intends to eat pareve foods with meat – cook them in a meat pot; and when one intends to eat them with milk – cook them in a dairy pot. This is in order to prevent confusion, for if people become accustomed to eating with milk pareve foods that were cooked in a meat pot, it is possible that they will sometimes err and think that a meat food is pareve and come to eat it with milk, or the reverse. There is also a concern that sometimes the utensils will not be cleaned well, and then there is a remote concern that the pareve food will absorb taste from the pot and become meaty or dairy, and people will mistakenly eat it with the other kind.</p><p>However, when necessary, it is permitted to cook a pareve food in a clean meat pot of metal or glass in order to eat it afterwards with milk, or the reverse. Likewise, when necessary, it is permitted to cut sharp foods such as onion and garlic with a meat metal knife that was cleaned well, for a dairy dish, or the reverse. Admittedly, some are stringent and forbid eating with milk a pareve food that was cooked in a meat utensil, or the reverse. However, since it is clear beyond any doubt that after glass and metal utensils are cleaned with soap they do not emit tastes (below 32:6; 8), the halakha follows the lenient opinion (below 32:11-12).</p><p>But if the pareve food was cooked in a meat or dairy metal or glass pot that was not cleaned well with soap, the poskim disagree as to whether it is permitted to eat the pareve dish with the other kind. Likewise, if the pareve food was cooked in a meat or dairy earthenware utensil, even though it was cleaned well with soap, since earthenware absorbs tastes into itself, the poskim disagree as to whether it is permitted to eat it with the other kind. <sup>8</sup></p><p>A wooden or plastic cutting board absorbs tastes; therefore one should designate a special board for meat foods and a special board for dairy foods. And one who wishes to cut foods for both kinds should also designate a board for pareve food. Be-di'avad (after the fact), if vegetables were cut on a meat cutting board that was cleaned well, and they were mixed into a dairy dish, the dish is kosher. But if the vegetables were sharp, such as onion, and the dish did not contain sixty times the onion, the dish should be forbidden to be eaten, lest a taste of milk [?] entered it through the onion. In a pressing situation one may be lenient (below 32:12).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"This law is explained at length below 32:11, note 14. In brief, there are three opinions in this law: <b>1) The custom of some of those of Sephardic descent</b>, following Rabbi Yosef Karo in Bedek Ha-bayit: it is permitted le-khatḥila to cook a pareve dish in a meat utensil that is ben yomo (used within the past twenty-four hours) in order to eat it with milk, or the reverse. <b>2) The custom of most of those of Sephardic descent</b>, following what is explained in SA 95:1: le-khatḥila it is forbidden to cook a pareve dish in a meat utensil that is ben yomo in order to eat it with milk, or the reverse, and if the utensil is not ben yomo it is permitted. <b>3) According to the custom of those of Ashkenazic descent</b>: even in a meat utensil that is not ben yomo one does not prepare pareve food in order to eat it with milk, or the reverse. Be-di'avad, if one has already cooked it, it is permitted when necessary to eat it with the other kind. And if the pot was ben yomo, even when necessary one is not lenient in this, and only be-di'avad, if it has already been mixed, is the food not forbidden.<br>This dispute exists today with regard to earthenware and wooden utensils, and metal and glass utensils that were not cleaned well with soap. But with metal and glass utensils that were properly cleaned with soap, since it is known that no taste at all of the previous food remains in them, members of all communities may follow the lenient opinion, as explained below 32:11, note 14. However, le-khatḥila, in order to maintain the separation between meat and milk, people practise as explained above.<br>Likewise, regarding a meat knife with which a sharp food was cut, or a meat pot in which a sharp food was cooked, the Rishonim disagree as to whether the sharp food, because of its sharpness, absorbs the taste of the meat. And today, with metal and glass utensils that were properly cleaned with soap, one should rule according to the lenient opinion, as explained at length below 32:12, note 15."}]},{"slug":"17-25-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-09/","title_he":"ט – עירוב כלים","title_en":"Mixing Utensils","body_en":"<p>Even when one wishes to prepare a cold pareve food, it is proper to prepare it with a set of utensils of one kind, dairy or meat, in order to maintain the separation between meat and dairy utensils. For example, when preparing the pareve food in a meat bowl, it is proper to stir it with a meat spoon. However, when necessary, for example when one is in a hurry and there is no washed meat spoon there, one may stir the pareve food with a dairy spoon, or the reverse.<sup>9</sup></p><p>In kitchens with many workers, such as the kitchens of educational institutions, experience has shown that without setting strict procedures, the concern of confusion between meat and milk increases. All the more so when some of the workers are not meticulous in observing mitzvot, as in army kitchens and in the kitchens of event halls, hotels and restaurants. Therefore it is customary to set for public kitchens separation procedures that are much stricter than what is customary in home kitchens, and this is entrusted to the rabbis responsible for granting kashrut certificates, who, according to their experience and their acquaintance with the kitchen workers, set the separation procedures needed to maintain kashrut.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"In a pressing situation, it is permitted to use a utensil that is normally used for cold dairy foods for eating cold meat foods, or the reverse, provided that the utensil has been washed well (below 33:11). For according to the basic law, utensils that are used for cold food are koshered by rinsing (Avoda Zara 75b), that is, by cleaning through washing. If so, utensils intended for cold foods may be used both for meat foods and for dairy foods, provided that they are washed in between. However, the custom of Israel is to fortify the separation between meat and milk and to take care not to use the same utensils for meat and dairy. In addition, by maintaining the separation one avoids several concerns: the main one, lest the utensils not be cleaned well between the kinds. There is also a concern lest people come to use them with hot food, or lest foods remain in them for twenty-four hours, in which case, according to some poskim, the utensil absorbs by the law of \"kavush ke-mevushal\" (soaking is like cooking) (below 35:9). It is also possible that even the Sages who said that a utensil used for meat is koshered for dairy use by rinsing did not intend that the same utensil be used regularly for both kinds, but rather that if it happens that one needs to use them for the other kind, or in preparation for Pesaḥ – their koshering is by rinsing. And even if in their time people were sometimes lenient in this because of the high cost of utensils, today there is no room to be lenient in this, as is accepted today in most Jewish homes, where people customarily buy special utensils for Pesaḥ and rarely kasher ḥametz utensils for Pesaḥ. Therefore, only in a pressing situation may one be lenient and use a cold meat utensil for a dairy food, or the reverse. Not only that, but it is proper to separate between the utensils, not using meat and dairy utensils at the same time even while preparing a cold pareve food, in order to fortify the separation between the kinds. However, since the food is pareve and the utensils are washed, it is permitted when necessary, even for a minor need, such as when there is no washed utensil there, or when this is the most suitable utensil for the purpose."}]},{"slug":"17-25-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-10/","title_he":"י – כלי פרווה","title_en":"Pareve Utensils","body_en":"<p>By law there is no need to keep pareve utensils, for even when one wishes to cook a pareve food and eat part of it with meat and part of it with milk, one may cook it in a clean meat or dairy metal pot (as explained in halakha 8). However, in practice, many are accustomed to be meticulous and keep pareve utensils, in order to prepare in them foods part of which they wish to eat with meat and part with milk. One of the reasons for this meticulousness is the concern that sometimes the utensils are not cleaned well and a taste of meat or milk remains in them. This meticulousness has become more widespread as the prices of utensils have fallen.</p><p>On the other hand, some prefer from the outset not to keep pareve utensils, because confusion sometimes arises from them, for people are accustomed to be careful about the separation between meat and milk, but they are not accustomed to be so careful about pareve, and perhaps they will mistakenly cook a meat dish in a pareve pot and afterwards mistakenly mix it with milk, or the reverse. Also, people do not know whether to wash the pareve utensils with the meat utensils or with the dairy ones.</p><p>For those who are accustomed to use pareve utensils, if they erred and cooked a meat or dairy food in a pareve utensil, they kasher the utensil by hagala (koshering in boiling water) in order to restore it to pareve status. If boiling milk or boiling meat gravy spilled on it, one wipes off the gravy and pours boiling water on that spot, in order to keep it pareve.</p><p>By law it is permitted to wash pareve utensils in a dishwasher or in a sink together with meat or dairy utensils, and they remain pareve. However, when dealing with a poor dishwasher in which remnants of fats and foods remain, one should not wash pareve utensils in it together with meat or dairy utensils. Likewise in a sink, if the utensils are soaked there with very hot water, or for twenty-four hours or more, one should not soak pareve utensils with dairy or meat utensils. Indeed, this is one of the confusions that may arise from pareve utensils, that the clear separation between meat and milk becomes blurred, and therefore some are meticulous to always wash the pareve utensils separately.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-25-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-11/","title_he":"יא – טעויות ותקלות","title_en":"Mistakes and Mishaps","body_en":"<p>When cooking a pareve dish in a meat pot, one should not stir it with a dairy spoon, and likewise the reverse. If one erred and stirred, as long as the pot and the spoon were made of metal, and were clean before the cooking began, the dish is kosher and the spoon does not need koshering.</p><p>If one erred and stirred a meat dish with a dairy metal spoon, or the reverse: if the spoon was clean, be-di'avad the dish is kosher, since a clean metal spoon does not emit tastes. But the spoon needs koshering by hagala, because it was used once in boiling milk and once in boiling meat (below 32, note 11).</p><p>Sometimes one cooks on the stovetop at the same time a meat dish and a dairy or pareve dish in a dairy metal pot, and if gravy from the meat pot splashed onto the walls of the dairy pot, since the whole pot was boiling hot, the whole pot needs koshering by hagala. However, the dish inside it is kosher, since the taste does not pass to it from the splash through the metal.<sup>10</sup></p><p>If boiling meat gravy splashed onto the wall of a cold dairy metal pot, or the reverse, since the pot that received the splash was cold, only its outer layer was affected by the splash, and therefore there is no need to kasher all of it by hagala; rather it suffices to pour boiling water from a kettle on the spot where the gravy splashed, in accordance with the rule \"ke-bol'o kakh polto\" (as it absorbed, so it expels). This is how it is done: one wipes the gravy off the pot and pours boiling water on that spot. It is customary to pour water there abundantly, in a quantity sixty times greater than the outer layer of the pot at the spot where the gravy splashed, and when there is doubt whether the water is sixty times the outer layer of the pot at that spot, one mixes soapy water into the water (Peninei Halakha, Pesaḥ 10:10; below 33:4).</p><p>If one erred and cut boiling hot meat with a dairy metal knife, if the knife was clean in the usual manner, the meat is kosher, and the knife needs koshering by hagala (below 32:8). And if the meat was cold, the knife needs a good cleaning but does not need koshering.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"<b>Wooden and earthenware utensils</b>, and all other materials that absorb and emit tastes: if one cooked a pareve food in a meat wooden pot and with a dairy wooden spoon, both ben yomo (used within the past twenty-four hours), and the dish did not contain sixty times the pot and the spoon, the dish is forbidden, and the utensils need hagala. And if it contained sixty times what entered from the spoon into the pot, the dish is kosher, and the spoon needs hagala but the pot does not need hagala, since the taste that came out of the spoon was nullified in sixty. And if the pot or the spoon was not ben yomo, and the dish did not contain sixty times the pot and the spoon, the dish is kosher and the utensils need hagala (SA 94:1; 3-4). If one of the utensils that need hagala was earthenware, it has no remedy (below 32:10). An earthenware or wooden pot in which meat is cooked, and milk splashed onto its outer wall, as explained below 32, note 19: sometimes the dish too becomes forbidden and the pot needs hagala, and if it is earthenware, it has no remedy.<br>If one stirred a meat dish with a dairy wooden spoon that was ben yomo, and the dish did not contain sixty times more than what entered from the spoon into the pot – the dish is forbidden and the spoon needs hagala. And if the dish contains sixty times, or the spoon is not ben yomo – the dish is kosher and the spoon needs hagala. If the spoon that needs hagala was earthenware, it has no remedy."}]},{"slug":"17-25-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-12/","title_he":"יב – מנהג שני כיורים ודין מדיח כלים","title_en":"The Custom of Two Sinks and the Law of Dishwashers","body_en":"<p>It is customary to install two sinks, one for washing dairy utensils and one for washing meat utensils. Likewise it is customary to designate one side of the counter for dairy foods and the other side for meat foods. Pareve foods may be prepared on both parts of the counter, provided that the counter is clean. When necessary, one may use one sink and one counter for both kinds, provided that one takes care to clean the sink and the counter of food remnants between one kind and the other.<sup>11</sup></p><p>It is customary to designate separate scrubbing cloths for dairy and for meat. However, by law, as long as soap is mixed with the water, it is permitted to scrub meat and dairy utensils with the same cloth, for the soap spoils the tastes of the foods in such a way that they are no longer considered meaty or dairy.</p><p><b>Dishwasher:</b> It is permitted to use the same dishwasher and the same racks for meat and dairy one after the other, while taking care not to wash them together, lest the tastes of meat and milk become mixed together before they are spoiled by the soap.</p><p>Some are accustomed to be meticulous and buy two racks, one for meat utensils and one for dairy utensils, in order to maintain the customary separation between meat utensils and dairy utensils, and some are meticulous to designate the dishwasher for one kind, and for the two kinds they install two dishwashers. However, as stated, according to the law, it is permitted to wash in the same dishwasher and on the same rack, once meat utensils and once dairy ones.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"In Minḥat Yitzḥak 2:100, he was stringent and required two sinks, because he was concerned for the view that pouring boiling water has the power to draw tastes out of one utensil and cause them to be absorbed in a second utensil (the view of the Pri Ḥadash and Pleiti), and consequently there is a concern that if boiling water is poured into the sink – it will draw out of the walls of the sink a taste of meat that will be absorbed into a dairy utensil, or the reverse. And according to Igrot Moshe YD 1:42, if people take care that the utensils do not touch the sink itself, for example by placing a rack in the sink, it is permitted le-khatḥila to use one sink, once for meat and once for dairy, because pouring does not have the power to draw taste out of the sink and cause it to be absorbed into the utensils without direct pouring onto the sink and the utensils while they are touching each other.<br>However, according to the rules of halakha, as long as people take care that utensils soiled with milk and with meat are not in the sink together, and that remnants of meat foods do not remain in it when dairy utensils are placed in it, or the reverse, there is no concern of a prohibition. And so in practice many God-fearing people have practised for many years. There are several considerations for this. <b>1)</b> According to most poskim, the tastes that may come out of the sink are \"nat bar nat de-hetera\" (a secondary, permitted taste), since they come out into the water before they meet the taste of the other kind (SA 95:3). <b>2)</b> Even if we are stringent in accordance with the Rema, who holds that tastes that come out of the wall of the sink and meet a taste of the other kind are not considered nat bar nat de-hetera (below 32, note 18), if there is soap there, there is no prohibition in this, and very often there is soap in the sink. <b>3)</b> Only if the water is at a heat of yad soledet bo will pouring have the power to act, whereas the water in the sink is generally not at such a heat. So wrote at length the Yalkut Yosef 89:80, and as explained in Yabi'a Omer 10, YD 4. <b>4)</b> Some say that hot water flowing from the tap in the sink has the status of flowing from a kli sheni (a secondary vessel), and consequently it does not have the status of pouring, and cannot cook to the depth of a peel (Or Le-Tziyon 3:10:11). <b>5)</b> Even if we say that water flowing from the tap has the status of pouring, since it has moved away from the source of heat, it is reasonable that it be considered pouring whose stream was interrupted, which does not cause absorbing and expelling at the same time (see below 35:3, note 3). <b>6)</b> All the more so when the sink is made of ceramic that is non-absorbent like glass, or of metal, in which tastes do not stick and are not absorbed (below 32:5; 32:9, regarding the law of porcelain-ceramic).<br>However, it is agreed that one should not put dirty dairy and meat utensils into the sink together, in order to maintain the separation between meat and milk, and also because of a remote concern lest a few remnants of meat and milk become mixed, and boiling water be poured over them, and they will be found to be cooked together to the depth of a peel and even render the utensils non-kosher."},{"n":12,"en":"In Minḥat Yitzḥak 2:100, he was stringent, in keeping with his view regarding the sink, that one dishwasher should not be used for meat and dairy except under very difficult conditions. In Igrot Moshe YD 2:28-29, he permits using the same dishwasher for meat and dairy because the tastes that come out of the food remnants are nullified in sixty, provided that one rack is designated for meat and one rack for dairy, lest food remnants of the previous kind remain on the rack and mix with the utensils of the other kind. In Yabi'a Omer 10, YD 4, he is lenient according to the basic law to wash meat and dairy utensils together, because whenever there is hot water in the dishwasher it is with soap, and consequently the tastes that may mix with one another are spoiled, as the SA 95:4 and most of the Aḥaronim there wrote, that it is permitted to wash dirty meat utensils in a dirty dairy cauldron with boiling water, provided that there is ash there that spoils the taste of the foods. Thus far. It seems that one should not wash meat and dairy utensils at the same time, because there is a reasonable concern that their tastes will become mixed before they are spoiled by the soap. On the other hand, it seems that even one who is careful to have two sinks is not obligated to be careful to buy two dishwashers or even two racks. First, because there is no binding custom to install two sinks, and according to the basic law one may use one sink for both kinds, as many have practised for years. Second, the separation of sinks is more important, because in the sink the utensils are sometimes left for a long time soaking in water, and the food remnants stuck to the utensils are not always spoiled there by soap, whereas in the dishwasher utensils are not left soaking in water.<br>Some are meticulous and are accustomed to clear out the food remnants in the filters, because of the concern lest their taste not be spoiled enough and remnants of meat and milk accumulate there. However, in practice, there is no need to be concerned lest the food remnants there were not spoiled.<br>When, based on experience, one finds that after washing very dirty utensils remnants of food and fat remain in the dishwasher (common in old dishwashers), one must take care after washing them to run the dishwasher again at the highest temperature in order to clean it well before washing utensils of the other kind."}]},{"slug":"17-25-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-13/","title_he":"יג – כיריים","title_en":"Stovetops","body_en":"<p>It is permitted to use the same grate for meat and for milk, because even if a little meat or dairy gravy boiled over onto the grate, the fire that prevails there burns and spoils what spilled. The same law applies to electric and ceramic stovetops: it is permitted to place on the same surface once a meat pot and once a dairy pot, since the heat of the stovetop burns what sometimes spills from them.<sup>13</sup></p><p>When cooking on the stovetop at the same time a meat pot and a dairy pot, one should take care that space remains between them, so that one dish will not boil over onto the wall of the other pot. And if the two pots touched each other during the cooking, as long as there was no moisture connecting them at the place of contact, the pots and the dishes are kosher, for tastes do not pass through dry utensils (Rema 92:8). And if there was moisture connecting them, for example because one dish boiled over, the dishes are kosher and the pots need hagala (as explained in halakha 11).</p><p>One should be stringent not to eat foods that fell onto the metal surface beneath the grate, because sometimes there are remnants of meat and dairy foods there. And if a thick piece fell there, one may cut off and throw away a thickness of about two centimetres from the side that touched the surface and eat the rest. And if it is known that the surface was cleaned well and is still clean, it is permitted to eat what fell on it, since the whole concern is about the grease on it, but there is no need to be concerned that the surface absorbed taste that it will later emit. Likewise, if a dairy food fell there, and it is known that since the last cleaning no meat food was cooked there, the dairy food that fell there is kosher (below 32:8-9).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"<b>Induction stovetops</b>: On these stovetops the surface on which the pots are placed is like non-absorbent glass; however, unlike ceramic stovetops, in which the source of heat is in the ceramic surface, with induction the source of heat is in the pot, which is heated by a magnetic field, and from the pot the heat spreads to the dish and to the surface on which it stands. Thus these stovetops do not have the full power to burn what boils over from the pots. In practice, if one is always careful to clean off the stovetop what boiled over onto it, one may heat on the same places once a meat pot and once a dairy pot, for the glass of these stovetops does not absorb, and in addition, all the contact there is from utensil to utensil. Those who wish to be meticulous should le-khatḥila designate one side for meat cooking and one side for dairy, and when necessary, they should clean the surface well and may then cook a meat pot on the dairy side as well, and the reverse. Admittedly, below 33:9 it is explained that if they were used for non-kosher food, they must be koshered according to the manner in which they absorbed; however, here, where meat and milk have not yet become mixed, there is no need to kasher but only to clean, so that they will not become mixed."}]},{"slug":"17-25-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-14/","title_he":"יד – תנור אפייה","title_en":"Baking Ovens","body_en":"<p>The custom of Israel is to fortify the separation between meat and milk, and accordingly many are accustomed to buy an oven with two compartments, and to designate one for meat and one for dairy, or to buy an oven with one compartment and designate it for one of the kinds only. One who wishes to use the same compartment once for meat and once for dairy may do so, provided that he has a special baking tray for meat and a special baking tray for dairy, and he takes care to kasher it when switching between the two kinds by heating the oven for about half an hour at the highest temperature, and it is also customary to clean it beforehand. And even those who are meticulous not to use the same oven compartment once for meat and once for dairy may do so when necessary.<sup>14</sup></p><p>Many are accustomed that when they wish to bake in a meat or dairy oven a pareve food that will be permitted to be eaten with both kinds – they first heat the oven at the highest temperature for about half an hour, in order to make it pareve. Those who wish to be lenient may bake the pareve food without koshering, for it is clear that there is no possibility at all that a taste of meat or milk will be sensed in the pareve food.</p><p>If one erred and cooked a dairy dish in a meat oven, be-di'avad the dairy dish is kosher, for in practice no real taste of meat entered the milk, and at most, if meat remnants remained in the oven, it is possible that vapours will rise that might give an odour of meat to the dairy dish, and be-di'avad odour does not render forbidden (see below 35:10). And the oven needs koshering by heating at the high temperature for half an hour.<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"It is possible that sometimes ten minutes suffice to burn away all the remnants of the tastes of the other kind; however, until the oven reaches the high temperature ten minutes and perhaps more may pass, and therefore it is customary to kasher it by heating at the high temperature for about half an hour."},{"n":15,"en":"Below 35:10, regarding the law of zei'a (condensation), that is, vapours that have condensed, it is explained that condensation renders forbidden only under very special conditions, such as the condensation that forms on the lid of a pot; therefore, according to the basic law, if a meat baked item and a dairy baked item were baked at the same time in one compartment, they did not become forbidden. And even if a meat dish and a dairy dish were cooked inside the oven in open pots, as long as a great deal of condensation did not pass from one pot to the other, they did not become forbidden. In cases of doubt, one should ask a Torah scholar.<br>Some say that it is permitted to bake and cook meat after dairy or dairy after meat in an oven without any koshering, since there is no need to be concerned about condensation remaining from the previous baking or cooking, since it is burned up during the baking and cooking. This is the opinion of Responsa Beit Ha-yotzer YD 20-21; Even Yekara 3:18; Yaskil Avdi 7, YD 4; Rav Kafiḥ, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 15:33.<br>And some say that it is permitted to cook and bake in the same compartment once meat and once dairy by koshering the oven in between by heating at the highest temperature, which is like libun kal (light koshering by fire), which eliminates the condensation that may have been absorbed in the walls of the oven. And even if food remnants remained, it burns them in such a way that no condensation that could render forbidden will come out of them. This is the opinion of Or Le-Tziyon 3:10:2; Si'aḥ Naḥum 47; Devar Ḥevron YD 2:88; Rav Toufik in Responsa Kol Eliyahu YD 2; Tefilla Le-Moshe 5:23. And so it emerges from the words of Minḥat Shlomo 2:67, and Mishneh Halakhot 16:10.<br>And some are stringent and hold that one should not use the same compartment once for meat and once for dairy, because of the concern lest condensation come out of the remnants of one kind and impart taste to the other kind, for sometimes dairy or meat substance spills there, and there is a concern that people will bake or cook the other kind without cleaning it. There is also a concern lest they forget which kind they cooked beforehand and not kasher. This is the opinion of the Maharsham 3:208; Ḥelkat Ya'akov YD 23; Minḥat Yitzḥak 5:20; Le-horot Natan 1:33-34. However, a central argument in their words was related to ovens in the past, in which many more remnants of the previous baking and cooking used to remain. In practice, after many tests, it has become clear that the concern they raised is unfounded, and it is not possible to sense taste from the condensation of the remnants of the previous food.<br>In practice, the halakha follows the middle position, that one may use the same compartment once for dairy and once for meat by koshering in between, as explained above. Admittedly, in Yabi'a Omer 5, YD 7, he added a condition of waiting twenty-four hours before koshering the oven, and he insisted on this mainly with a dish that produces a great deal of condensation. Something similar was written in Igrot Moshe YD 1:40; 59, and Mekor Ne'eman 1:587. However, the reason for waiting twenty-four hours is difficult, for koshering by heating the oven at the high temperature has the status of libun kal, which not only expels the tastes like hagala but also burns them, and therefore there is no need to wait twenty-four hours (see Peninei Halakha, Pesaḥ 10:5; below 33:7).<br>Many are accustomed le-khatḥila not to use the same compartment, by means of koshering, once for meat and once for dairy, in order to distance themselves from error and confusion. However, this custom is not obligatory, and one who wishes to kasher the oven at any time may do so. Between one kind and pareve – many are accustomed to kasher the oven by heating it at the high temperature for half an hour. However, by law there is no need to kasher it, as explained above in note 8 regarding the law of nat bar nat de-hetera.<br>An oven in which meat was baked and afterwards pareve: if the baking was at the highest temperature, even according to the custom of the meticulous it is permitted to bake a dairy baked item in it afterwards without additional koshering, and likewise the reverse, because heating the oven at the high temperature while baking the pareve koshers it for switching between meat and milk. If one erred and baked meat in a clean dairy baking tray, this is explained below 33:9, note 10."}]},{"slug":"17-25-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-25-15/","title_he":"טו – מיקרוגל","title_en":"Microwave Ovens","body_en":"<p>One may use the same microwave for dairy and meat foods while creating a separation between them. In the separation one must pay attention to two things: first, that dairy or meat foods not be placed directly on the same plate; second, that a large amount of condensation not enter the food being heated from the cavity of the microwave.</p><p>Therefore, one must take care not to place foods directly on the fixed plate of the microwave; rather, dairy foods should be placed on a dairy plate and meat foods on a meat plate, and these plates should be placed on the plate of the microwave. Likewise, one should designate a special cover for dairy foods and a special cover for meat foods. And although vapours come out through the small holes in covers intended for the microwave, the condensation that comes out of them does not have the power to accumulate on the walls and ceiling of the microwave and impart taste to it, and all the more so they do not have the power to draw out taste that may have been absorbed in the walls of the microwave and put it into the food being heated.</p><p>Alternatively, one may determine that the normal status of the microwave is dairy, and if one wishes to heat a meat food in it, one places another plate or another surface on the fixed plate of the microwave, and covers the meat foods with a cover or a box, or wraps them in a bag. One should do the same when heating a pareve food in order to eat it with meat foods.</p><p>A microwave that was used for non-kosher food must be koshered (below 33:9). Be-di'avad, when there is no time to kasher it, or when it belongs to people who are not careful about kashrut, one may heat foods in it by placing them on an additional plate and putting them inside a bag or box that will wrap them on all sides, even though an opening will remain there for vapours to escape.<sup>16</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"Some hold that the vapours coming out of the foods in a microwave do not condense to such a degree that they can impart taste, and therefore there is no need to separate between meat and milk (Si'aḥ Naḥum 48; Migdal Tzofim 4:31; Rav Halberstam). On the other hand, some hold that it is forbidden to use the same microwave for meat and milk, for there is a concern that the condensation there contains tastes, and there is a concern that the foods will not be covered as required to prevent condensation or the foods boiling over, since it is used frequently (Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv, as cited in Shevut Yitzḥak 6, chapter 5). The accepted ruling is as explained above.<br>Regarding the plate, some wrote that according to the Sephardic custom the glass plate does not absorb, and it suffices to clean it between meat and milk (Yalkut Yosef), whereas according to the Ashkenazic custom and the Ben Ish Ḥai, one must create a separation, since it absorbs. And according to what is explained below 32:5, according to all opinions one must be stringent as explained above, since we are dealing with a level close to a kli rishon (see Hora'a Berura 108:1, Bi'urei Hora'a s.v. \"ela\").<br>Be-di'avad, if one heated without a cover as required, the food is kosher. And if there was also no separation between the food and the plate, and it is possible that the plate was slightly soiled with remnants of non-kosher food or of the other kind, the food that was heated on this plate is not kosher. And if it is thick and lean, that is, without grease, one should remove from it the depth of netila, that is, a thickness of about two centimetres (1.9 cm), from the parts that came into contact with the plate."}]}]});
